; io — the console, at the only two types every host agrees on.
;
; THE LIBRARY LAYER, not the target's. `go/fmt` names Go's `fmt` package and
; claims nothing beyond that host; `io` names an interface several targets share,
; and this file is Go's cell of it. `(provides T M decl...)` is exactly
; `(target T (module M decl...))` written where the library lives -- a target
; FRAGMENT (target-system.md 7.2, 8), and the lowest layer, because a library's
; opinion about a host loses to that host's own files.
;
; WHY IT IS NOT `fmt.Println`. Go's own name is variadic `...any`, and `any` is
; where the agreement stops: a float prints `1e+06` on Go and `1000000` on V8,
; so a portable `(print x)` would be a Tier 1 name that is not Tier 1. The two
; types below ARE portable and were measured to be, not assumed —
; string-literals.md checked every one of the 1,112,064 scalar values, and
; integers.md found all four hosts agree on decimal rendering inside ADR 0012's
; window. So the signature is the claim, and the checker refuses the rest.
;
; `go/fmt` still declares `Println` and everything else Go's `fmt` has. This
; module is not a replacement for it and does not wrap it: both emit the same
; call, and neither is lowered further than the host provides.

(provides go io
    (prim print-line ((s string)) string stmt "fmt.Println(%s)" (import "fmt"))
    ; ---- and the same without the newline
    ;
    ; A program that BUILDS its whole output as text — jsonfmt and freq both do —
    ; supplies its own line breaks, so a `print-line` here would add one the
    ; program did not write. Two names rather than a flag, because a flag would
    ; be a value at run time where this is a choice at the call site.
    (prim print ((s string)) string stmt "fmt.Print(%s)" (import "fmt"))

    (prim print-int ((n int)) int stmt "fmt.Println(%s)" (import "fmt")))
